
                 ----------------------------------------
                 >>> DOCUMENTS FOR OFFLINE-TOOL V1.00 <<<
                 ----------------------------------------

                          Released at 13-Nov-1991


   Not depending on the size of your Hard-Disk, it becomes full someday,
   the smaller HD you have got - faster it will be filled up. You probably
   do not enjoy very much updating older files on your upload dir to  
   another dir.  Well, this tiny tool for Ami-Express bbs will do deleting
   and dir updating for you. 


   Note:
   -----

   You must have files "paths" , "NDirs" and of cource dir#'s themselves!
   All Ami-Express SysOp's do know what these files actually do on bbs
   system, so there's no need to explain why these files must be there.


   Usage:
   ------

   OffLine <Conf> <Days#/Megs#> <Ask> <Over> <Mf> <Sf> -Options

   OffLine ? for HELP screen.

   Please note:    You may insert all the things in command line in any
		   order except <Conf> otherwise Offline too won't work
		   properly!

  'Conf'  is full path of conference which you want to procees.

  'Days#' here you put the number of days of files you wish to be deleted
          from your dir and HD. Certainly this works backwards. hah! The
	  number of days is given right after Days and must be 1-99 or 
	  otherwise OffLine won't execute this option.

  'Megs#' number of Mega Bytes you want to get free space on your HD.
          Calculating will be started from oldest files to newer ones.
	  The number of Megs is given right after Megs and must be 1-99,
	  just like in Days option.

	  Note: One MegaByte = 1024*1024 bytes (= 1048576 bytes)
                calculation will continue as long as next file
                to be caltulated doesn't raise the sum over meg#
                you have specified!

   'Ask'  You will be asked (Y)ES,(N)O,(A)BORT,(S)KIP ASK before deleting
	  each file from your HD. That's good if your aren't 100% sure you
	  don't loose anything really important. Of Course you see the whole
	  discription of file you're going to delete with this option!
	  All outputting will be done on own window. If OffLine fails in 
	  opening it (in the lack of memory or something) then all output
	  will be pointed to your CLI-window.

	  (Y)es      = Yeah ! Delete file. (Just press enter !!! or type Y)
	  (N)o       = No !! Don't delete !! Just skip to next file.
	  (A)bort    = Abort the whole deletion.
	  (S)kip Ask = Fuck Asking !! Just delete as wanted !!

	  Note: ask mode works only with Days or Megs option.

   'Over' If this option is chosen then all new DIR's will be written
	  over old DIR's on your HD or what so ever. If not chosen new
	  DIR's will be created on RAM: disk from where you got to copy
 	  them in their right places. RAM disk is used, because there
	  is always a little chance that something goes wrong... Do not
	  use this option until you are familiar with OffLine-tool !!! 

   'Mf'	  With this cool option you may mark AMI-FAKED files as 'FAKED!'
	  in your upload dir. If this option is not set then all AMI-
	  FAKER files will be shown as ONLINES.

   'Sf'	  If you have specified option Megs or Days then this option will
	  skip all AMI-FAKED files in your upload dir. Some SysOp's may
	  want to leave faked files to their upload dir to remind users
	  that uploading FAKE files is unforgivable.

   Options: -O   Just marks all unavailable files as 'OFFLINE' to your
                 upload directory.
            -C#  Move all unavailable filenames (+discriptions) to
                 directory #.
	
		 Note: if you don't use any number after 'C' Offline-
		 tool puts all the unavailable stuff to the next lower
		 directory.

		 Note2: if the number is greater or equal to Upload dir
		 number, then all unavailable stuff will be put to the
		 next lower directory.

		 Note3: if no option is specified, then option -O is
		 used.

   Examples:  OffLine BBS:Elite Days5 Ask Over Mf Sf -C2
              OffLine BBS:Demos Days3 -O
              OffLine BBS:Elite Megs10 Ask -C1
	      OffLine BBS:Demos (=OffLine BBS:Demos -o)

